agents-results/2026-08-31/problem-intelligence-plane/evidence/a4-remediation/reviews-bdd83e9/returns/A4-review-luna.json:15:      "detail": "For COMMIT_INTENT, recovery selects the target only when current_key_sha256 equals target_key_sha256; otherwise it accepts the old generation when current_key_sha256 equals old_key_sha256. On the first generation both values are null when the signing-key file is absent, so an ambiguous missing-key state is treated as old. Recovery then restores old_literature_files, old_state, and old_authentication. With no prior state this removes the target literature and returns to a new empty state, then _prepare_locked removes the journal at lines 1083-1088. A crash or durable key-install loss after the target generation was otherwise committed can therefore lose the target transaction without a retained recovery marker. Missing authority must fail closed or remain recoverable; it cannot be used as proof of the old choice."
agents-results/2026-08-31/problem-intelligence-plane/evidence/a4-remediation/reviews-bdd83e9/returns/A4-review-luna.json:22:      "detail": "_remove_transaction unlinks the journal before fsyncing the root directory. If unlink succeeds but that directory fsync raises, the surrounding storage lock still releases through its context-manager finally blocks. An independent LiteratureBase writer can then see no pending journal and append literature that is absent from the transaction target snapshot. If the uncommitted deletion later becomes visible again after a crash, recovery uses the target snapshot and deletes that writer's files. No reviewed test injects failure between unlink and durable directory deletion."
agents-results/2026-08-31/problem-intelligence-plane/evidence/a4-remediation/reviews-bdd83e9/returns/A4-review-luna.json:52:    "This independent transaction/concurrency review found P1 implementation defects in missing-key recovery and journal-retirement fencing.",
