+      "title": "OR-based persisted lookup can commit identity-drifting duplicate evidence",
+      "detail": "_persisted_observation_for_input selects candidates matching either observation_id or idempotency_key and then chooses by status and lexicographic observation_id. The two identities are not interchangeable: LiteratureBase rejects an observation_id reused for another logical source, and the state validator requires the persisted record selected for duplicate evidence to have the input idempotency key. _save_state authenticates the state but does not run the full state validator before committing it.",
+      "reproduction": "Persist observed OBS-A for URI /a and content a and OBS-B for URI /b and content b. First process OBS-B so its key is in seen_observation_keys, then submit an input with observation_id OBS-A but URI /b and content b. The seen-key path at lines 882-898 returns DUPLICATE with OBS-A because lines 1037-1057 select the ID match. The current process persists successfully. A new runner rejects the state with 'duplicate item evidence idempotency key conflicts' at lines 2321-2327. This is deterministic durable identity drift from valid SourceObservation input, not a stale-contract or acceptance-rebinding issue. The alternate persisted-ID tests cover a non-ambiguous key match and do not cover this cross-match."
+    },
+    {
+      "severity": "P1",
+      "path": "matharc/v02/artifact_store.py",
+      "line": 136,
+      "title": "The commit can retire its only recovery journal before literature data is durable",
+      "detail": "ArtifactStore.put_bytes writes the blob and replaces it without fsyncing the blob or its containing directory, save_manifest does the same for manifest.json, and LiteratureBase._record does the same for observations.json at lines 411-417. TopicObservationRunner._save_state snapshots those files, writes and fsyncs the topic transaction/state/authentication/key files, verifies the in-memory snapshot, and then _remove_transaction retires and removes the journal at lines 2582-2591. A root-directory fsync for the topic files is not a durability barrier for the literature files or their nested directories.",
+      "reproduction": "Use the import path that writes an artifact and both literature manifests, then model power loss after the topic state, authentication, signing key, and COMMIT_INTENT/retirement directory entries are durable but before the literature blob/manifest file data and parent directories are durable. On restart the journal is gone, while the authenticated state names a literature snapshot whose files may be stale or missing; recovery has no target snapshot available and the literature/state binding fails or has already lost the imported data. The focused suite checks recovery deletion fsync calls and journal retirement, but injects no power-loss boundary around the import writes."
+    },
+    {
+      "severity": "P1",
+      "path": "matharc/v02/topic_observation.py",
+      "line": 1611,
