29987:+      "result": "FAIL reproduced; duplicate identity state is accepted in-process then rejected on restart, and a failed marker restoration opens the external literature writer"
29990:+  "findings": [
30004:+      "detail": "ArtifactStore.put_bytes writes the blob and replaces it without fsyncing the blob or its containing directory, save_manifest does the same for manifest.json, and LiteratureBase._record does the same for observations.json at lines 411-417. TopicObservationRunner._save_state snapshots those files, writes and fsyncs the topic transaction/state/authentication/key files, verifies the in-memory snapshot, and then _remove_transaction retires and removes the journal at lines 2582-2591. A root-directory fsync for the topic files is not a durability barrier for the literature files or their nested directories.",
30005:+      "reproduction": "Use the import path that writes an artifact and both literature manifests, then model power loss after the topic state, authentication, signing key, and COMMIT_INTENT/retirement directory entries are durable but before the literature blob/manifest file data and parent directories are durable. On restart the journal is gone, while the authenticated state names a literature snapshot whose files may be stale or missing; recovery has no target snapshot available and the literature/state binding fails or has already lost the imported data. The focused suite checks recovery deletion fsync calls and journal retirement, but injects no power-loss boundary around the import writes."
30012:+      "detail": "_remove_retired_transaction unlinks the retiring marker, fsyncs the topic root, and on fsync failure attempts to restore the marker. Restoration errors at lines 1623-1629 are swallowed before the method raises. The storage locks then unwind, and LiteratureBase._topic_transaction_pending sees no active or retiring marker, so an independent writer is allowed to mutate literature while the commit outcome remains unresolved.",
30013:+      "reproduction": "In a temporary directory, inject an OSError for the root fsync after the retiring marker is unlinked and independently fail _atomic_write_bytes before it can restore that marker. The runner raises the expected durable-retirement error, but the marker is absent after lock release and a separate LiteratureBase.import_bytes returns IMPORTED. If the non-durable unlink later reappears after a crash, recovery can apply the transaction snapshot and discard that external import. test_retirement_fsync_failure_keeps_external_writer_fenced only exercises successful marker restoration."
30020:+      "detail": "The 66 focused tests pass, including the alternate persisted-ID replay test and the retirement-fsync test, but they do not combine an observation_id collision with a different persisted idempotency key, do not validate state before _save_state commits it, do not inject failure of retiring-marker restoration, and do not exercise literature blob/manifest fsync ordering through a crash boundary. Passing tests therefore do not establish the claimed identity and durability remediation.",
30024:+  "verdict": "FAILED",
30025:+  "proposed_state": "FAILED",
30030:+    "No real power-loss or filesystem-device fault test was run; the literature durability finding follows from the write and fsync ordering in the frozen source.",
